acme_ops: FYI our /invoke calls started 400ing around 2026-04-02T14:21Z. Validator errors cite additionalProperties "debug_info" and enum mismatch for "mode" (we used "express"). Getting retries and higher p50/p95 latency. Repro below.

acme_ops: ```json
{
  "tool": "payments.compute",
  "account_id": "acct-9001",
  "mode": "express",
  "lines": [{"sku": "sku-1", "qty": 1}],
  "debug_info": {"sampling": true}
}
```

jamie: can you paste the exact API response and request id?

acme_ops: response: ```json
{
  "error": "schema_validation",
  "details": [
    {"path": "mode", "message": "value \"express\" not in enum"},
    {"path": "debug_info", "message": "additional property not allowed"}
  ],
  "request_id": "req_xc9f21"
}
```

matt: checked router trace for req_xc9f21. Our shallow discriminator runs against per-tool top-level args. Unknown top-level keys make the expected candidate fail early (additionalProperties), router tries fallbacks and sometimes misroutes to a generic handler -> 400 or wrong tool. That causes extra hops and adds to latency.

matt: router log excerpt:
``
[2026-04-02T14:21:12Z] req=req_xc9f21 router: candidate=payments.compute valid=false reason=additionalProperties(debug_info); enum_mismatch(mode=express)
[2026-04-02T14:21:12Z] req=req_xc9f21 router: fallback=generic-handler validation_failed
```

jamie: short-term mitigation: can you remove top-level debug_info and use an X-Client-Debug header or namespaced meta key (e.g., meta.debug) and set mode to "normal" or "priority"?

acme_ops: our JS SDK adds debug_info by default for dev tracing. We can change it but that touches many customers. Prefer server-side tolerance if safe.

matt: agreed. Two options: 1) SDK change (safer long-term), 2) gateway sanitizer to drop shallow unknown top-level keys before discriminator (fast, low-risk). Avoid making all tool schemas additionalProperties:true.

matt: example Node middleware to drop unknown top-level keys for /invoke:
```js
// strip-topkeys.js
const ALLOWED = new Set(['tool','account_id','lines','mode','request_id']);
function stripTopKeys(req,res,next){
  if(req.path !== '/invoke') return next();
  const body = req.body || {};
  Object.keys(body).forEach(k => { if(!ALLOWED.has(k)) delete body[k]; });
  req._sanitized = true; // log later
  next();
}
module.exports = stripTopKeys;
```

sara: I opened a focused PR to add a narrow sanitizer: https://github.com/redwood-inference/gateway/pull/4120. It strips only shallow unknown top-level keys for the /invoke endpoint and emits a metric (gateway.dropped_topkeys) with request_id and dropped_keys list. Planning canary in ap-northeast-1.

deploy-bot: canary rollout started for gateway/sanitizer-pr-4120 in ap-northeast-1 (rollout r-20260402-11)

sara: canary up at 2026-04-02T14:24:50Z. Watching dropped_topkeys, 4xx-rate, and tool-misroute counts.

matt: also improved enum error messages to include allowed values so clients get immediate guidance.

acme_ops: we re-sent the same payload (no SDK changes). Through the canary region the call succeeded, no schema 400, latency back to expected. Verified request_id req_xc9f21 now completed successfully. Thanks! :tada:

jamie: marking resolved for the customer. Follow-ups: 1) land sanitizer across region after audit, 2) add integration test with extra top-level props, 3) publish SDK compatibility note and migration guidance.

sara: opening follow-up tickets: INT-221 (sanitizer audit), SDK-88 (migration guide), TEST-501 (integration test). Also propose a long-term fix: enumerate allowed top-level keys per-tool in the model catalog and derive the sanitizer list from that.

acme_ops: appreciate the quick turnaround. We'll keep monitoring for 15m and report if anything else shows up.

matt: noted. Closing thread after monitoring window unless you see new failures.
